# ESP32-P4 target overrides — assumes an ESP32-P4 + ESP32-C6 devkit of the
# ESP32-P4-DevKit-A / ESP32-P4-WIFI6-DEV-KIT class: N16R32 (16 MB flash,
# 32 MB PSRAM), C6 co-processor wired to the P4 over SDIO.
#
# What is different about this target, in one paragraph: the P4 is the fastest
# chip osynth runs on (360-400 MHz dual RISC-V *with* an FPU, against the S3's
# 240 MHz Xtensa) and it has USB-OTG and a full-duplex I2S port, so audio and
# USB are strictly better than the S3. What it does not have is a radio. BLE
# therefore runs the NimBLE *host* here and its controller on the C6, with
# ESP-Hosted carrying HCI over the SDIO link — see the BLE block below.
#
# THE PIN CHOICES BELOW ASSUME A MODULE-ON-CARRIER BOARD, not a full devkit.
# On the board this was set up for, the header exposes only GPIO1-5, 20, 32,
# 33, 45, 46, 47 plus a pair of pads labelled ES_I2C_SDA / ES_I2C_SCL (the
# on-board codec's control bus), and the micro-SD socket is already wired on
# the board rather than brought out. Everything else — USB 24/25, console
# 37/38, the SDIO link to the C6 on 14-19 + 54, the SD socket — is spoken for
# in copper. That leaves eleven pins for the whole analogue front end, which
# is why the I2S block sits at 32/33/45-47 here and not at the 16-20 the S3
# uses or the 22-28 an earlier revision of this file picked for a devkit.
# On a board with a wider header, any free pins work: all five are routed
# through the GPIO matrix.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_OSYNTH_FLASH_16MB=y

# Chip revision, and the CPU clock that follows from it. **These are one
# setting, not two.**
#
# ESP32P4_SELECTS_REV_LESS_V3 says the image targets P4 revisions 0.x/1.x,
# which is what a devkit of this class carries (v1.3 on the board this was
# brought up on). IDF 6.0 defaults it off with REV_MIN v3.1, and a v3.1 image
# is refused at boot by a v1.x chip — so it is pinned here rather than left to
# whatever the toolchain's default happens to be that release.
#
# The revision then decides which CPU frequencies exist at all, because the
# CPLL is capped at 360 MHz below rev 3.0 and runs at 400 MHz at and above it.
# The two sets do not overlap: rev <3 offers 90/180/360, rev >=3 offers
# 100/200/400 (XTAL divisors either way). Kconfig does not enforce the pairing
# in the direction that bites — ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_400 carries no
# `depends on`, so asking for 400 MHz alongside REV_LESS_V3 configures and
# builds cleanly, then dies before app_main in a boot loop with
#
#     assert failed: esp_clk_init clk.c:104 (res)
#
# which is rtc_clk_cpu_freq_mhz_to_config() refusing a frequency the silicon
# cannot synthesise. That is exactly what CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_400
# did on this board. 360 MHz is the top of what rev <3 offers, and still well
# clear of the render-budget floor described in sdkconfig.defaults.
#
# **On a rev 3.x board both lines change together**: drop REV_LESS_V3 (and let
# REV_MIN default to v3.1) and take MHZ_400. Leaving 360 behind there asserts
# in exactly the same place, for the mirror-image reason.
CONFIG_ESP32P4_SELECTS_REV_LESS_V3=y
CONFIG_ESP32P4_REV_MIN_100=y
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_360=y

# PSRAM. The symbols differ from the S3's: the P4 has one line mode (HEX,
# 16-line) rather than OCT/QUAD, and its speed choice tops out at 250 MHz with
# 200 MHz the default. SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND keeps a board whose PSRAM does
# not come up booting — the looper and the large FX buffers then fall back the
# same way they do on an S3 without PSRAM.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_HEX=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_200M=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND=y

# Loop-persistence flash backend: same warning as on the S3 — do NOT enable
# SPI_FLASH_AUTO_SUSPEND. See the long note in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3.
# CONFIG_SPI_FLASH_AUTO_SUSPEND is not set

# External I2S DAC is the master out, as on the S3.
CONFIG_OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC=y

# ===========================================================================
# BLE with no radio on the die (ESP-Hosted -> ESP32-C6)
#
# ble_ctrl.cpp is unchanged on this target: it only ever calls NimBLE *host*
# APIs, and IDF does not gate BT_ENABLED / BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED on
# SOC_BT_SUPPORTED — only BT_CONTROLLER_ENABLED. So the host stack compiles
# and runs here exactly as it does on the S3.
#
# What changes is underneath it. BT_CONTROLLER_DISABLED makes the bt component
# compile the host with *no* HCI transport at all (components/bt/CMakeLists.txt
# only adds hci_uart.c/hci_h4.c when BT_NIMBLE_TRANSPORT_UART is also set), and
# ESP-Hosted then supplies the transport, shipping HCI over the same SDIO link
# it uses for Wi-Fi. Hence TRANSPORT_UART=n: this is the VHCI arrangement, not
# a second dedicated UART to the C6.
#
# A link failure naming ble_transport_init / ble_transport_to_ll_cmd /
# ble_transport_to_ll_acl means these four hosted symbols are not all set —
# those are the functions the transport is supposed to provide.
#
# The C6 must be flashed with the matching ESP-Hosted co-processor firmware.
# Host and slave are versioned separately; ble_ctrl logs the co-processor
# version at boot precisely so a mismatch is visible before it is mysterious.
# ===========================================================================
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLUEDROID_ENABLED=n
CONFIG_BT_CONTROLLER_DISABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_NIMBLE_TRANSPORT_UART=n

# ESP-Hosted itself. ESP_HOSTED_ENABLED gates the component's *entire*
# CMakeLists — sources and public include dirs alike — so with it off the
# component builds empty and the only symptom is
# "fatal error: esp_hosted.h: No such file or directory". Its only default-on
# rule is ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_ENABLED && ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_LIBRARY_HOSTED, hence the
# Wi-Fi-remote pair below even though osynth never brings Wi-Fi up: that layer
# is weak-linked and costs nothing at runtime if esp_wifi_* is never called.
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_LIBRARY_HOSTED=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_ENABLED=y

# Which chip is on the other end. ESP_HOSTED_CP_TARGET_* depends on the
# matching SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_*, which esp_wifi_remote defines (IDF 6.0.1 has no
# components/esp_wifi/remote). C6 is already the default when the host is a
# P4; pinned here so a config regenerated without the dependency resolved
# fails loudly instead of quietly picking something else.
CONFIG_SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_CP_TARGET_ESP32C6=y

# SDIO GPIOs. The choice defaults to "No development board", which leaves
# every pin at a generic default; FUNC_BOARD is the P4+on-board-C6 wiring and
# is the right starting point for a P4/C6 devkit. **If the co-processor never
# answers at boot, this is the first thing to change** — either to another
# member of the choice or to ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_NONE plus explicit
# CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_* pins from the board schematic.
# The transport itself needs no line here: ESP_HOSTED_HOST_INTERFACE already
# defaults to SDIO for a P4 host with a C6 co-processor.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_FUNC_BOARD=y

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_ENABLE_BT_NIMBLE=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_NIMBLE_HCI_VHCI=y

# Transport buffers out of internal RAM and into PSRAM. Not a tuning knob —
# without it this target does not boot.
#
# ESP-Hosted takes every transport mempool from internal DMA-capable RAM, 64-byte
# aligned: one pool per registered channel (SDIO_TX_Q_SIZE 20 + 5 padding blocks
# x 1600 B ~= 40 KB each, transport_drv.c) plus the SDIO driver's own
# 31 x 1536 B ~= 47 KB (sdio_drv.c). Against the ~408 KB of internal RAM this
# build has at heap_init, the pools run the region dry during startup — before
# app_main, so before osynth has allocated anything at all — and the last one
# out asserts:
#
#     E (1612) HS_MP: mempool create failed: no mem
#     assert failed: sdio_mempool_create sdio_drv.c:258 (buf_mp_g)
#
# a boot loop with no osynth code involved. MEMPOOL_PREFER_SPIRAM makes those
# allocations try DMA-capable PSRAM first and fall back to internal on failure,
# which the P4 supports: SOC_SDMMC_PSRAM_DMA_CAPABLE is 1, so the SDMMC
# peripheral can DMA straight to and from PSRAM.
#
# Keep it on here even if the pools are made to fit: internal RAM is the budget
# osynth actually competes for — render_init() takes the audio buffer pool from
# MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL (graph_render.cpp) and runs *after* all of this. The cost
# is transport latency on a link carrying BLE HCI, which is control traffic, not
# audio.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_MEMPOOL_PREFER_SPIRAM=y

# Transport pins are deliberately NOT set here. ESP-Hosted brings its own
# CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_* defaults, which match Espressif's P4+C6 reference
# wiring; a third-party board may route the SDIO link differently. If the
# co-processor never answers at boot ("hosted co-processor did not answer"),
# this is the first place to look — menuconfig -> Component config ->
# ESP-Hosted. Pin them here once the board's wiring is confirmed, so the
# setting survives a clean build.

# ===========================================================================
# "osynth Synthesizer" options that are target-specific on the ESP32-P4.
# Commented out at their Kconfig default; see the header in sdkconfig.defaults
# for the syntax and for the target-independent options.
#
# Already set above and deliberately left alone: OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC (the
# DAC is the master out) and OSYNTH_FLASH_16MB (the flash size the loops
# region is cut from — the other members are CONFIG_OSYNTH_FLASH_8MB=y and
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_FLASH_32MB=y, each of which also wants a matching
# CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_* so the image header matches the chip).
# ===========================================================================

# --- I2S analogue front end -------------------------------------------------

# Same choice as on the S3 — DISCRETE (PCM5102A, optionally + PCM1808) or
# ES8388 — and the same trade: the discrete DAC sounds better, the codec is
# one chip for both directions.
#
# The pin budget does not decide it. Both cost the same five GPIOs, because
# the PCM1808 shares BCLK and WS with the PCM5102A, and the codec's two extra
# wires come off the board's ES_I2C pads rather than the header. What decides
# it is that those pads are there at all: a control bus with pull-ups already
# fitted is exactly what the ES8388 needs and what the discrete pair has no
# use for. Two boards and two power rails against one, for a converter a step
# behind — take the discrete front end here only if the line output is what
# the instrument is judged on.
#
# NOTE ON THE ON-BOARD 3.5 mm JACK: it is NOT the codec being configured here,
# and enabling ES8388 does not make it work. Boards in this class carry an
# **ES8311** — a different (mono, 1-in/1-out) codec with a different register
# map, which osynth does not support: components/codec/ implements ES8388
# only. The ES8388 below is an external module wired to the header.
#
# The two coexist, which is the point of the arrangement: they share the
# control bus (ES_I2C, below) but not the I2S port, and the ES8311 is never
# written, so it stays in its power-up state — unconfigured and silent. It
# does answer the address probe, so expect it in the boot-time bus scan
# (log_bus_scan, codec_es8388.cpp): 0x18 is the board's own ES8311, 0x10 or
# 0x11 the external ES8388, 0x33 the M5 module's STM32, 0x40/0x41 an ES7210
# mic array if the board has one. Only the 0x10/0x11 answer is the one the
# codec driver needs; the rest are the board identifying itself.
# DISCRETE on this target, against the S3's ES8388, and not on merit: the
# ES8388 front end is unusable here. An M5Stack M144 that plays cleanly from the
# S3 is grossly distorted on the P4, identically across two modules, and the
# investigation in private_docs/P4_AUDIO_DISTORTION.md eliminated the entire
# digital domain — render chain verified at the USB tap, the chip's own emission
# verified at the pad by the loopback self-test, codec registers verified by
# dump, and every timing/framing/drive property of the link swept without
# effect. Unresolved, and parked pending measurement rather than closed.
#
# A PCM5102A sidesteps all of it: no I2C, no MCLK, and BCLK at half the rate.
# Switch back by swapping these two lines once the ES8388 fault is understood.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_FRONTEND_DISCRETE=y
CONFIG_OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388=y

# Stereo line input on the DAC's own port. The capability is here — the P4's
# I2S is full duplex and its MCLK routes through the GPIO matrix, which is the
# pair of properties the classic ESP32 lacks — but the front end above decides
# whether it costs anything. **Currently off**, because Kconfig defaults it to n
# on a discrete front end: capture there needs a second chip (a PCM1808) and two
# more pins, where on the ES8388 the ADC is already in the package.
#
# So there is nothing to set here today. If the ES8388 is ever restored above,
# this goes back to defaulting y and DIN/MCLK below come live with it — which is
# exactly the transition the pin note further down is about.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN=y

# Stream to USB *and* the I2S DAC at once. Default y, as on the S3.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_USB_AUDIO_TAP=y

# --- I2S pins ---------------------------------------------------------------

# All five pins, on the header described at the top of this file. **Check them
# against your own schematic before wiring** — a different carrier exposes a
# different set, and nothing here is forced by the silicon.
#
# Why 32/33/45-47 out of the eleven available:
#
#   45, 46, 47  are SD1_CDATA4-6 in the IO MUX, i.e. the 8-bit-mode data lines
#               of SDMMC slot 1. A micro-SD socket only has four, so a board
#               that wires its slot to that IOMUX (39-42 + CLK 43 + CMD 44)
#               has these three left over — which is exactly why a board with
#               an on-board reader can still bring them out. Free, adjacent,
#               and the natural home for the three clock-rate signals.
#   32, 33      plain digital (their alternate functions are EMAC RMII and
#               SPI2 HOLD/WP, neither in use), so they take the two data lines.
#
# What is deliberately left alone: GPIO2-5 are the JTAG pads MTCK/MTDI/MTMS/
# MTDO, and GPIO1/20 are the only other spare pins on the header — those six
# are worth more as serial MIDI in, encoders and buttons than as a home for a
# 12.288 MHz clock. None of the eleven is a strapping pin (on the P4 those are
# 34-38, all of them off-header here).
#
# An earlier revision of this file put the block at 22-28, which is right for a
# devkit with a wide header and wrong here — none of those pins are brought
# out on this board. The reasoning for moving off the *Kconfig* defaults still
# stands and still applies to any P4 board:
#
# The Kconfig block at 16-20 lands on top of the SDIO link to the C6. ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_FUNC_BOARD puts that link on
# CLK[18] CMD[19] D0[14] D1[15] D2[16] D3[17] (+ slave reset 54), so WS took
# SDIO CLK, DOUT took SDIO CMD and BCLK took D3 — and the two lines CMD52 needs
# were the two that collided. main.cpp brings I2S up before ble_ctrl_init(), so
# by the time ESP-Hosted reached the bus the I2S peripheral was already driving
# it, and card init failed fifteen times over:
#
#     E sdmmc_io: sdmmc_io_reset: unexpected return: 0x108
#     E H_SDIO_DRV: card init failed
#
# 0x108 is ESP_ERR_INVALID_RESPONSE, and the distinction matters when reading
# this log: a co-processor that is merely absent or unflashed answers nothing
# and times out with 0x107, which sdmmc_io_reset() explicitly tolerates
# (sdmmc_io.c:70). INVALID_RESPONSE means something was driving those lines.
#
# The SDIO side cannot move — the C6 is soldered down and that wiring is fixed
# in copper. The I2S side goes to a converter you wire by hand, so it is the
# side that gives way. Pins to stay away from on this chip: **GPIO24/25 are the
# USB-OTG full-speed PHY** (D-/D+ — the port the synth enumerates on, and see
# usb_dev.c on why it is that port and not the high-speed one), GPIO37/38 are
# console UART0, GPIO10-13 are the UART1 IOMUX pins, GPIO14/15 the LP-UART, and
# 14-19 + 54 are the SDIO block above. If you change the ESP-Hosted board choice
# to match a different schematic, re-check these against the new SDIO block.
#
# **MCLK and DIN are set explicitly, and must stay that way on this target** —
# even though, with the discrete front end selected above and the line input
# off, Kconfig does not currently emit either symbol and both lines are inert.
# Leaving a value on an unused pin is free; discovering the collision below the
# hard way a second time is not.
#
# The collision: turning the ES8388 back on brings both pins live at their
# Kconfig defaults of 16 (SDIO D2) and 20, and 16 is inside the C6's SDIO block.
# Audio comes up first, ESP-Hosted then fails card init with
# `sdmmc_io_reset: unexpected return: 0x108`, BLE never appears, and nothing in
# the log names a pin. Only 46/47/32 are actually driven today.
#
# DO NOT MOVE THIS BLOCK TO GPIO1-5. It was tried, on the reasoning that those
# are the board's other free header pins, and it does not merely fail to help —
# it takes the codec off the bus. The ES8388 answered its probe, logged "muted
# at 0x10 ahead of init", and then NACKed step 2 of the init table, with a bus
# scan finding 0x18 (the carrier's own ES8311) and 0x33 (the M144's STM32) but
# no 0x10 at all. audio_io_start() runs between those two points, so starting
# the port on 1-5 is what removed it: the module still had power, the bus was
# healthy, and specifically the codec was gone.
#
# Whether that is a wiring slip or a carrier that uses GPIO1-5 for something is
# unresolved. Note the trap either way: M-Bus 1/3/5 are grounds and P4 GPIO
# 1/3/5 would be MCLK/LRCK/ASDOUT, so a crossed wire drives an output straight
# into ground.
#
# The open problem this was an attempt at: the same M144 plays cleanly on the
# ESP32-S3 target and is grossly distorted here, with everything else
# eliminated by direct evidence — registers dumped and verified, samples
# bit-perfect at the USB tap, distortion invariant across the whole out.level
# range, invariant into 32R and high-Z loads, invariant at 48 kHz and 24 kHz
# (which rules out marginal timing and ringing, but NOT a hard electrical fault
# on one pin), AUDIO_VDD steady at 2.80 V, all three grounds connected, both
# positions of the module's S1 strap, and BLE off so the C6's SDIO link is not
# running. Same firmware, same module: the pin numbers are what is left.
#
# 45/46/47 are the suspects. They are SD1_CDATA4-6, the 8-bit-mode data lines
# of the SDMMC slot this board's SD socket sits on, and "a 4-bit socket leaves
# them spare" is an assumption about the carrier rather than something read off
# its schematic.
#
# Next experiment, when there is appetite for it: move MCLK *alone* to GPIO20,
# one wire and one line. 20 is the only spare pin here with any evidence behind
# it — it is the serial-MIDI RX default, so the project already treats it as an
# ordinary GPIO — and MCLK is both the fastest signal and the one whose
# corruption best fits the symptom. One signal at a time from now on.
#
# MCLK: ES8388 MCLK, 12.288 MHz at 48 kHz. Mandatory even in slave mode, and
# unused entirely by a PCM5102A — inert while the front end is discrete, so
# GPIO45 is free for a UI encoder or serial MIDI as long as it stays that way.
CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_MCLK_GPIO=2
# ===== GPIO46/47 ARE NOT USABLE AS OUTPUTS ON THIS CARRIER =================
#
# Measured, not inferred. With the converter disconnected and the synth playing,
# a DC meter on the P4's own header reads:
#
#   GPIO32 (DOUT)  0 V on silence, 1.62 V while notes play   <- correct
#   GPIO46 (BCLK)  0.6 V, static, notes or no notes          <- not driving
#   GPIO47 (WS)    0.6 V, static, notes or no notes          <- not driving
#
# GPIO32 is the control: the loopback self-test proved it emits at the pad, and
# it measures correctly on the same meter in the same session. So the method is
# sound and 46/47 really are held. 0.6 V is not a dead output — 0 V or 3.3 V
# would be — it is a line clamped by something on the board, which the "free
# header pins" listing at the top of this file never accounted for.
#
# This is the root cause of both converter failures on this target, and the one
# fault that explains both symptoms: a PCM5102A must recover its whole clock
# from BCK through an internal PLL, so degraded clocks give it *silence*; an
# ES8388 has its own MCLK and converts regardless, so it made sound but sampled
# data against the same bad clock — which is the distortion that
# private_docs/P4_AUDIO_DISTORTION.md spent a week eliminating everything else
# for.
#
# GPIO45 is held too, and it was briefly BCLK here on the reasoning that it must
# drive because it had carried 12.288 MHz MCLK to the ES8388. That inference was
# wrong: the pins are *loaded*, not dead, so a codec with its own clock
# reference sort-of works on them while a PLL will not lock — which is exactly
# the pair of symptoms this board produced.
#
# OSYNTH_GPIO_OUTPUT_SCAN (sink_i2s.cpp) then measured every candidate by
# driving it high with nothing connected:
#
#   3.3 V, usable:  1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 20, 32, 33
#   held, unusable: 45, 46, 47
#
# So the shortage was imaginary — eight pins work, and the three the file
# originally picked are the only three that do not. GPIO2-5 are the JTAG pads
# (fine as GPIO, costs external JTAG) and 20 is the serial-MIDI default, which
# is why BCLK takes GPIO1: it is the one free pin with no other claim on it.
#
# **This very likely also explains the ES8388 distortion**, which was never
# root-caused: that front end had MCLK on 45, BCLK on 46 and WS on 47 — all
# three held — with only DOUT and DIN on good pins. Retrying it with all five
# signals on measured pins (MCLK 2, BCLK 1, WS 33, DOUT 32, DIN 3) is the
# obvious next experiment once the discrete DAC is confirmed working.
CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_BCLK_GPIO=1
CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_WS_GPIO=33
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_BCLK_GPIO=45   <- held at 0.6 V, does not drive
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_BCLK_GPIO=46   <- held at 0.6 V, does not drive
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_WS_GPIO=47     <- held at 0.6 V, does not drive
# DOUT: PCM5102A DIN, or ES8388 DSDIN.
CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_DOUT_GPIO=32
# DIN: PCM1808 DOUT, or ES8388 ASDOUT. Only with line input on — so inert
# today, and GPIO33 is free alongside GPIO45 while the front end is discrete.
CONFIG_OSYNTH_I2S_DIN_GPIO=3

# --- ES8388 codec (only with OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388) ------------------------
#
# LIVE again. This is the settled configuration for the M5Stack M144 —
# differential input, OUT1, CTIA — and every value in it was established the
# hard way; see private_docs/P4_AUDIO_DISTORTION.md.
#
# The codec came back once the I2S block moved off GPIO45/46/47 (which do not
# drive on this carrier) and onto measured pins, and once
# OSYNTH_CODEC_INIT_BEFORE_I2S was set for this target — with the port running,
# this board's control bus answers the address probe and then NACKs the first
# register write. Both were needed; neither alone is enough.

# Control bus — the codec's CDATA/CCLK, wired to the board's ES_I2C pads.
#
# **Confirm these two numbers against the schematic.** 7/8 is the Kconfig
# default for this target and where boards in this class usually route the
# on-board codec's bus, but the silkscreen says ES_I2C rather than a GPIO
# number, and that pair is the one thing here that cannot be moved to suit:
# it is fixed in copper on the board's side.
#
# Sharing it with the on-board ES8311 is fine and is the intended arrangement:
# osynth is the only master, and the addresses do not collide (0x18 against
# 0x10/0x11). The pads carry the board's own pull-ups, so do not add the 1k
# pair the ES8388 datasheet asks for on a bare chip — osynth's internal
# pull-ups (~45k) are already in parallel with them and a ready-made codec
# module brings its own as well (2.2k on the M5Stack M144).
CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SDA_GPIO=7
CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SCL_GPIO=8

# Analogue input pair and the M5 module's own switches. Set for the same
# M5Stack Module Audio (M144) the S3 target drives — this is the board that
# module gets moved to, not a second one, so the settings move with it. The
# long-form reasoning for each is in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3; the short
# version is that the M144's jack is wired differentially, and a single-ended
# selection there reads exactly half the amplitude on one channel rather than
# failing outright.
#
# On any other ES8388 board (ESP32-A1S, LyraT, "audio kit") drop these three
# and take LINE2 instead, which is the line/aux jack on all of them.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE2=y
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE1=y
CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_DIFF=y
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_M5_LINE_MIC=n

# HP_OMTP is deliberately left at its Kconfig default of n (CTIA) here, and
# that is a *departure* from sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3, which sets it y.
#
# It is not an input-only setting, which is how it got carried over. The mux it
# drives swaps which of the headset jack's two rear contacts (HP_P1/HP_P2 in
# M144_sch_moduleaudio_v10.pdf) reaches MIC_MONO_IN and which becomes ground —
# so it decides the *return path* of anything plugged into that jack, not just
# where a headset microphone lands.
#
# Ordinary 3-pole stereo headphones are tip=L, ring=R, sleeve=GND. Under OMTP
# the sleeve is muxed to MIC_MONO_IN, which reaches AGND only through R8/R9
# (22k each), so both channels return through 22k and through each other. The
# result is not silence — it is quiet, distorted, note-correlated noise, which
# is exactly how this presented on the P4 bring-up and is easy to read as a
# clocking or format fault. The `M5 module 0x33 after:` line in the boot log is
# what names it: hp-mode 0x01 is OMTP, 0x00 is CTIA.
#
# Set it y only for an actual OMTP-wired 4-pole headset (M5's own examples do,
# because they are demonstrating the headset microphone).
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_M5_HP_OMTP=y

# Output pairs. OUT1 for the M144, whose LOUT2/ROUT2 go nowhere — halves the
# volume-register writes per out.level. BOTH is the answer on an unknown
# board; OUT2 is the speaker-amp input on most audio kits.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT_BOTH=y
CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT1=y
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT2=y

# Input channel balance, both in the 0.5 dB ADC digital volume and in the 3 dB
# analogue PGA. 0/0 rather than the S3's values: the imbalance they correct is
# a property of the module's analogue wiring and should carry over unchanged,
# but it was measured on the S3 and nothing has measured it here yet. Read
# `in pk L/R` from the heartbeat with a mono source before setting either —
# see sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3 for the procedure.
#
# Commented out, not set to 0: both symbols `depends on
# OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN`, which is off above, so Kconfig does not emit
# them and a value here is an unapplied setting rather than a choice. The
# Kconfig default is 0 anyway — uncomment along with the line input.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_ADC_BALANCE=0
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_ES8388_PGA_BALANCE=0

# --- Looper persistence -----------------------------------------------------

# loop.save / loop.load. Default y (needs PSRAM, which this board has plenty
# of — 32 MB against the S3's 8).
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_LOOP_PERSIST=y
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH=y
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_SD=y

# --- SD card ----------------------------------------------------------------

# CONFIG_OSYNTH_DRUM_SD_KITS=n
# Shared by the SD looper backend and SD drum kits; also board-specific, and
# a P4 devkit usually has its own SD slot already wired somewhere else.
#
# ON A BOARD WITH THE SOCKET ALREADY FITTED, these four defaults are wrong and
# the card will not mount. osynth's backend is SDSPI (loop_store.cpp), while an
# on-board micro-SD socket on this chip is almost always wired to the SDMMC
# slot-1 IOMUX: D0-D3 on 39-42, CLK 43, CMD 44. The defaults below land three
# of four pins on that block but in the wrong roles, which is worse than not
# wiring it at all.
#
# It does not need header pins to fix. SPI2 routes through the GPIO matrix, so
# the firmware can reach pins the header never brings out, and every SD card
# supports SPI mode — the socket just has to be driven with the SD-to-SPI role
# mapping instead of the SDMMC one:
#
#   CS   <- DAT3 (42)      SCK  <- CLK (43)
#   MOSI <- CMD  (44)      MISO <- DAT0 (39)
#
# **Unverified on this board** — that is why it is commented out and the
# storage backend is left on flash. Two things to check on the schematic
# before trying it: that the socket really is on the slot-1 IOMUX, and that
# DAT1/DAT2 are pulled up rather than left floating (SPI mode leaves them
# unused, and a floating DAT1 is the card's interrupt line). If a buffer or
# level shifter sits between the P4 and the socket, check it is bidirectional
# on the data lines before driving them as SPI.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_SD_CS_GPIO=42
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_SD_SCK_GPIO=43
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_SD_MOSI_GPIO=44
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_SD_MISO_GPIO=39

# --- Serial MIDI ------------------------------------------------------------

# Rarely wanted here — USB-MIDI comes free with the USB-OTG peripheral. The
# Kconfig default (21) is not on this board's header; 20 is, and is one of the
# six pins the I2S block deliberately left free.
# CONFIG_OSYNTH_SERIAL_MIDI_RX_GPIO=20
